# Blueprint: Rolling restarts

Illustrative example of the required shape. Its content is not researched.

## Frame
Audience: solutions engineers who deploy apps but have not had to explain restart behaviour to a customer.
Purpose: field enablement
Time: 25 min
Format: workshop
Scenario: Hypothetical service restart during a busy period.
Task: Explain whether the proposed restart preserves serving capacity.

## Sizing
One lesson. Rollback is outside scope.

## Objectives
### O1 — Describe what a rolling restart does [recall]
### O2 — Explain when a rolling restart keeps capacity [explain]
### O3 — Handle the objection that rolling restarts guarantee zero downtime [handle]

## M1 — Rolling restarts (25 min)
Objectives: O1, O2, O3
- K1.1: A rolling restart replaces instances one batch at a time. [R:availability]
- K1.2: Capacity holds only when more than one instance is running. [R:availability] [R:instance-min]
- K1.3: Health checks decide when the next batch starts. [R:health-checks]
- K1.4: Rolling restarts give zero downtime for every app type. [unsupported]
Misconception: "Rolling always means zero downtime." Correction: a single-instance app still drops requests while it restarts. [R:zero-downtime]
Hook: A customer wants to restart during a busy period. What must remain available?
Example: A three-instance web app restarted one instance at a time while it serves traffic. Show a simulated status line: A serving, B restarting, C serving.
Ask: Which instances can serve this request? What changes if B is the only instance? Listen for: A and C remain available in the example. A sole restarting instance leaves no serving capacity.
Activity: 5 min. In pairs, change the service to one instance and draft a customer response. Output: a capacity prediction and its condition. Good answer: identifies the missing serving instance and avoids promising uptime.
Presenter handoff: Show status, ask, then debrief the single-instance case.

## Starting point
Prerequisite: Read status.
Likely mistake: Count B as serving.
Opening check: Which instances can serve now?
If missed: Trace one request to a serving instance before continuing.

## Shared case
Basis: K1.1, K1.2
Situation: Simulated restart during a busy period.
Observations: A serving, B restarting, C serving.
Decision: A and C retain capacity in this example.
Feedback: B is restarting. Trace the request again using only serving instances.
Variation: B is now the only instance.

## Job aid
Use when: Assess serving capacity during a restart.
Step: Identify the batch restarting. [K1.1]
Step: Count the instances still serving. [K1.2]
Evidence: Inspect current instance status. [K1.2]
Exception: A sole restarting instance leaves no serving capacity. [K1.2]

## Assessment plan
- O1: Check: What happens to an app's instances during a rolling restart? | Observe: States that instances are replaced in batches.
- O2: Check: Why does a single-instance app lose capacity during a rolling restart? | Observe: Explains the more-than-one-instance condition.
- O3: Check: A customer says rolling restarts guarantee zero downtime. What do you say? | Observe: Corrects the claim without dismissing the customer and agrees a next step.

## Practice plan
- main: roleplay. Objectives: O1, O2, O3. A sceptical platform lead who saw dropped requests last week and was told that could not happen.

## Open gaps
- K1.4: no evidence was found for the zero-downtime claim, so the training does not teach it.
- Rollback after a failed restart is not covered; the research question is still pending.

